From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 04:15:37 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 42/42] hw/input/tsc2100: Clock steadily when the audio timer
 runs late

When its timer ran late the codec caught up with as much as 100 ms of
frames in one go, more than two of the stock driver's 8 KiB periods.
The DMA then ran into the next period before the period interrupt was
handled, and the handler, which busy-waits for the next period to have
just started, timed out ("timed-out wating for DMA to start"). A real
codec doesn't catch up: clock at most 4 ms at a time and let the rest
slip, as an overloaded host loses sound rather than bursting it.

Booting the stock firmware in real time on a busy host, the timeouts
went from 2 to 1 (that one at a host stall the kernel reports as missed
timer ticks).

Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5Fc77Z6fVVe4rWJxPT
---
 hw/input/tsc2100.c | 14 ++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/input/tsc2100.c b/hw/input/tsc2100.c
index 1480d8dde..a7010db55 100644
--- a/hw/input/tsc2100.c
+++ b/hw/input/tsc2100.c
@@ -334,10 +334,16 @@ static void tsc2100_audio_tick(void *opaque)
     uint64_t due = muldiv64(now - s->clock_start, s->rate,
                             NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND) - s->clocked;
 
-    /* After a long gap (the machine stopped), start afresh. */
-    if (due > s->rate / 10) {
-        s->clocked += due - s->rate / 10;
-        due = s->rate / 10;
+    /*
+     * A real codec clocks steadily: when the timer runs late (a busy host,
+     * the machine stopped), clock at most a few milliseconds and let the
+     * rest slip. Catching up in one go would move several of the guest's
+     * DMA periods at once, while its interrupt handler, which the stock
+     * driver busy-waits in for the next period to start, can't run.
+     */
+    if (due > s->rate / 250) {
+        s->clocked += due - s->rate / 250;
+        due = s->rate / 250;
     }
     tsc2100_audio_clock(s, due);
     s->clocked += due;
